@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-long-rental-discount-10-off-at-3-days-rounded-to-the-nearest-cent
+type: decision-log
+title: "Long-rental discount: 10% off at 3+ days, rounded to the nearest cent"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+The desk now rewards customers who rent for longer: "a rental of 3 days or more now costs 10% less than
+its full price", shown in both `quote()` and `book()`. `dailyPrice` and `total` are integer euro cents
+(dl-001), so a 10% cut can land on a fractional cent (e.g. 3 days at 1501/day: full price 4503, 90% of
+that is 4052.7) and a rounding rule was needed that the request itself didn't specify.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Threshold**: "3 days or more" is `days >= 3` (a `LONG_RENTAL_DAYS` constant), using the whole-day
+  count `wholeDaysBetween` already validates and returns (dl-001/dl-002); no new period semantics were
+  introduced.
+- **Rate**: a flat 10% (`LONG_RENTAL_DISCOUNT = 0.1`) off the full price (`days * dailyPrice`), with no
+  tiering for longer rentals, matching the request as given.
+- **Rounding**: the discounted total is rounded to the nearest cent with `Math.round`, since `total` is an
+  integer number of cents everywhere else in the service and `Math.round` is a deterministic, pure
+  function of its input (no wall-clock or randomness), consistent with the determinism directive.
+- **Single point of calculation**: a `priceFor(days, dailyPrice)` helper is used by `quote()`; `book()`
+  already computes its `total` by calling `quote()` internally, so it inherits the discount without
+  needing its own copy of the rule.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Quoting and booking the same request will always agree on `total`, since both resolve through
+  `priceFor` via `quote()`.
+- Rounding to the nearest cent means two different items priced to produce the same pre-rounding total
+  could very rarely round to totals one cent apart from a naive expectation; this is accepted as standard
+  monetary rounding behaviour and is deterministic and reproducible in tests.
+- A future tiered-discount schedule (e.g. a larger cut for a week or more) would extend `priceFor` rather
+  than add a second discount path.
